this is all that remains of nasser  mohelis's home in mikaela in northern  sudan the walls and roofs collapsed  after heavy rains and flash floods in  river now state leaving him and his  family with nowhere to go  we lost everything there's nothing left  we have to start from scratch we need a  lot of things food clean water tends for  shelter any kind of assistance we can  get we feel like we're being ignored and  it could rain again at any time  at least three thousand homes have been  damaged by the floods in mikaela this  week the government has declared the  affected areas disaster zones kalimat  anjali and her 16 year old daughter have  been sleeping out in the open after  their home was washed away she's  struggling to adapt to their new reality  we need food and something to drink but  we also need a concrete place to stay  our house is completely destroyed and we  use beds to make a shelter for my  disabled daughter from the sun and the  wind but the beds won't protect us from  the rains to come  the floods have also destroyed shops so  people can't replace their possessions  they've appealed to the government for  assistance  however aid is yet to arrive many of  these families have been relying on  donations of food and clothes from other  families the government has set up  emergency centers but those here say the  response is too slow  town's sewage system is damaged  prompting fears of an outbreak of  water-borne diseases  the damage to the sewage system is worse  than the shortage of food and water the  stagnant water will bring mosquitoes and  we don't have mosquito nets so we hope  the government sends health supplies to  prevent the outbreak of disease  more rain is forecast for the coming  days people here say without help the  already dire situation will only get  worse heber morgan al-jazeera michaelab  river now state
 

